<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#osco-umbrian-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osco-Umbrian languages</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sabellic languages</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Osco-Umbrian languages, also called Sabellic, are a group of extinct Italic languages once spoken across central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The best known are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a>, used by the Samnites and their neighbours, and Umbrian, preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iguvine-tablets">Iguvine Tablets</a>. They were written in alphabets derived from Etruscan as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek letters, and gave way to Latin after the Roman conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osco-umbrian-languages.jpg" alt="Osco-Umbrian languages" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbachmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Age_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Following a theory of Antoine Meillet, the group was long treated as a branch of the Italic languages together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Faliscan. Alois Walde, Vittore Pisani and Giacomo Devoto proposed instead two distinct Indo-European branches, a view that gained some acceptance in the later 20th century, but the unitary theory of a single common ancestor remains dominant. The languages probably spread with successive arrivals of Indo-European peoples from the east, the Osci and Umbri reaching the peninsula after the Latins and Falisci but before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iapygians">Iapygians</a>, such as the Messapians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> was spoken in the south, in Samnium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a>, as well as by the Mamertines at Messana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>; it was the language of the Samnite tribes, powerful enemies whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took years to subdue. Umbrian lay to the north, and between them lay poorly attested languages such as Volscian, Sabine, Marsian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-picene-language">South Picene</a>, which may have formed a dialect continuum with features of both. The languages were fusional, with seven cases in the singular of nouns, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><p>Where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> kept the labiovelars, they merged them with the labials, so that Latin quattuor answers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> petora, four; and the aspirates that Latin shows as b, d or h between vowels all appear as f, as in Oscan mefiai beside Latin medius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The languages are known almost entirely from a few hundred inscriptions dating from 400 BCE to the 1st century CE, among them many dedications and signs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a>, and from some loanwords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Umbrian is the best known because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iguvine-tablets">Iguvine Tablets</a>, a single text of about 4,000 words. Sabellic was first the collective name of the Italic peoples of central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the time of Roman expansion; Theodor Mommsen used it in his Unteritalische Dialekte for the dialects that were neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> nor Umbrian, and it now names the whole group.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osco-Umbrian languages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They spoke an <b>Osco-Umbrian</b> language known only from glosses.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a></li><li><blockquote>They spoke Umbrian, an <b>Osco-Umbrian</b> language.</blockquote><a href="/state/umbrians">Umbrians</a></li><li><blockquote>The Volsci spoke an <b>Osco-Umbrian</b> language and appear in the late 6th century BCE moving from the Apennines into the Lepini mountains, the Liri valley and the Pontine plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a></li></ul></section>
